<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adam of Bremen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1085</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> was a German chronicler who wrote the Deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, a history of the archbishopric of Hamburg-Bremen, around 1075. Its fourth book is the most important early description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the Baltic, including the pagan temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> and peoples such as the Sambians. He drew on information from the Danish king Sweyn Estridsson.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen.jpg" alt="Adam of Bremen" width="300" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam of Bremen († 1081/1085) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Utdrag_ur_Gesta_Hammaburgensis_ecclesiae_pontificum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archbishop Adalbert (1043–1072) hoped to make the see a northern patriarchate, and around 1075 the chronicler <b>Adam of Bremen</b> recorded its history and the geography of the North.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to <b>Adam of Bremen</b> his son Sweyn Forkbeard rebelled and drove him into exile, where he died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Christianisation was slow: <b>Adam of Bremen</b> described a pagan temple at Uppsala in the 1070s, and the archbishopric of Uppsala was founded only in 1164.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…myths as history in Latin, including the story of Amleth, the original of Shakespeare’s Hamlet; the German chronicler <b>Adam of Bremen</b> described the temple at Uppsala around 1075; and the Arab envoy Ibn Fadlan described the ship funeral of a chieftain of the Rus on the Volga in…</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>For the West Slavs between the Vistula and the Elbe, the chronicles of Thietmar of Merseburg, <b>Adam of Bremen</b> and Helmold, the lives of Otto of Bamberg, Saxo Grammaticus’ Gesta Danorum and the Knýtlinga saga describe temples and cults in detail.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Adam of Bremen</b>, writing about 1075, described a temple at Uppsala with images of Thor, Odin and Freyr.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.16,13.48&amp;year=1075" aria-label="Meissen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4856 391 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adam of Bremen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5306 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5306 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.16,13.48&amp;year=1075">What was here in 1075? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
